well a vector is represented by an arrow  and it has both magnitude which is  represented by its length how long the  vector is and also by the direction  which is you know the angle which way  the vector is pointing or which way the  force is being exerted for example so  here we have two points in the  coordinate plane and we want to find the  vector that goes from this point to this  point so if I represent that as a vector  okay which is just drawn as an arrow  okay this is called the initial point  starting point this is called the  terminal point where it terminates or  stops and to find the component form of  a vector what you want to do is you want  to take the terminal point minus the  initial point so I'll just write that  down here so a terminal point where it  terminates or stops minus initial so  where it initiates or begins so what we  would do is we would say 7 minus 2  three minus one so all I did is I  subtracted the X's and I subtracted the  Y's and if we simplify we get five comma  two and that just means that you're  going right five in the horizontal  direction up to and that's your vector  right there so five two and notice we're  using these triangular brackets just to  differentiate from a coordinate point  where we'd have the the parentheses the  curved brackets so this is a vector this  is component form and again with these  vectors what you can do is if I took  this vector and I drew it down here or I  drew it over here these vectors would be  equivalent to one another because they  both all of them have the same  horizontal distance and vertical  distance they're both going right five  up to right five up to right five up to  what you don't want to do is you don't  want to make the vector longer these  would not be equivalent vectors and you  don't want to change the angle or the  direction of the vector these would not  be equivalent vectors so as long as it  has the same component form the vectors  are equivalent just meaning that you  know the forces exerted the same  direction with the same amount of force  or magnitude so this has been how to  find a component form of a vector I'll  see you in the next video
 

